<br /><br />
After Lord Maha Vishnu slayed Kroni into six pieces, ‘Neettiya Yuga’ ended. In subsequent Yugas, in order to save Dharma which were under threat by the asuras born, Lord Maha Vishnu annihilated several asura forces born from the flesh pieces of slayed Kroni. He killed demon Gundomosali in ‘Chatur Yuga’, Thillai Mallaan and Mallosi Vahanan in ‘Nedu Yuga.  In ‘Kretha Yuga’ the fourth yuga, the two demons Singha Mugha and Soorapadma who carried boons of invincibility, when ruled the universe with tyranny, Lord Vishnu incarnated as Lord Muruga and annihilated them with his trident. Since the Yuga had few more years to vanish, Singha Mugha was made to take birth as Hiranya who was also annihilated by Lord Vishnu in the form of Lord Narasimha who was neither human, nor in animal form, and killed him with his strong nails without using any weapon. He killed him on the doorstep of the palace at ‘Sandya Kal’ meaning twilight period when Sun fades away and Moon raises.  In the fifth of the Yuga called Thretha Yuga, demon Ravan who carried three and half crores (3½) of rare boons was killed by Lord Maha Vishnu, in the form of Lord Ram. 
<br /><br />
In the sixth of the Yugas called ‘Dwapara Yuga’, Lord Maha Vishnu incarnated as Lord Krishna and attempted to reform evil minded Duryodhana by way of counselling.  When Duryodhana refused to take the good advice of the Lord and waged war, Lord Krishna was left with no option but to side with Pandava brothers to annihilate the evil Duryodhana and made Pandava brothers’ the rulers of the land.  Lord Krishna realized that the purpose of his incarnation had been completed and decided to return to Srirangam shedding the body of Lord Krishna. In order to end the incarnation, he enacted a drama for his own disappearance by resting in a forest area, when a misunderstood hunter’s arrow pierced through the foot of the Lord, thus making the mortal body fall lifeless. Lord Krishna who was residing inside the body immediately quit the body of illusion and proceeded to his abode in Srirangam. 
<br /><br />
On way to Srirangam, when he reached the hillside of mountain Arigona, he saw seven celestial maidens namely Maragatham, Valle, Valli, Saligai, Sarakathakanni, Sarithai and Arimadavam, all residents of mount Kailaya. The maidens used to come there every day, played for some time in the forest grove, took bath in the river and collected sacred water in their hands to anoint their Lord Shiva by way of worship.  When Lord Maha Vishnu went and stood before them in the form of a mendicant, they could not recognise him and gave way to the mendicant to move, but Lord Maha Vishnu in disguise of a mendicant, asked them to offer worship to him too since they had done enough service to the Lord of braided hair, meaning Lord Shiva. The maidens turned down his request conveying that they cannot even think of offering worship to anyone other than their own eternal Lord sitting on the taurine (Nandi), let them be anyone- Deva Rishis, Maharishis or even Lord Thirumal, meaning Lord Vishnu without knowing that the mendicant was none but Lord Maha Vishnu in disguise. 
<br /><br />
Angered by their refusal to offer worship, Lord Maha Vishnu threatened that they would not be able to carry the water from Ganges anymore to anoint their Lord.  Immediately he brought the entire grove under the grip of unbearable chillness and stood there in the form of a fire after absorbing seven forms of semen from seven worlds of the universe.  Unable to bear the extreme chillness, the maidens went near the fire and stood around it as if they were hugging the fire, unaware that the fire was Lord Vishnu in disguise. Instantly they lost their modesty, and gave birth to seven children.  Since they lost their modesty, they could not collect water in their hands to anoint their Lord. Heartbroken celestial maidens felt ashamed that since they had lost their chastity, they would not be able to collect water anymore to anoint their Lord.  Therefore, leaving the children born to them lying there, they went to the forest and sat in penance with plea to get married to the same mendicant who outraged their modesty. 
<br /><br />
<div style="padding-left:50px;">
