Once, Lord Vaikundar came out of the seashore, after suitably advising all those assembled in the seashore, he walked towards Swamithoppu from Tiruchendur. On his way, the Lord established a Shivalinga, in a place called Moolakundapathi, also known as Ambalapathi, offered prayers to it and proclaimed that he would come back again. 
<br /><br />
On reaching Manavaipathi in Swamithoppu, Lord Maha Vishnu instructed Kalaimuni (Rishyasringa) to send communique to the ruler of Kali, the reasons for his incarnation and his preaching. Without wasting time immediately Maharishi proclaimed to the universe through a message saying ‘since Lord Narayana himself has incarnated as Lord Vaikundar, in the southern end in the month of Masi, in the year 1008, no one should pay offerings, be a pole bearer, or feel fear of other divines, but at the same time remain honest, free of corrupt practices. 
<br /><br />
In one of the earlier Yugas, Arjuna sat in penance in Tamaraiyur Pathi in Poovandar Thoppu (Thoppu meaning grove) seeking Pasupatham (Most powerful weapon in the arsenal of Lord Shiva). News flashed all over the land that Veera Narayanar (Vaikundar) was in penance under a mango tree in the same place where Arjuna had earlier penanced. Lord Vaikundar called the demons and ghosts and warned them to keep away from the worldly affairs as he was set to examine the state of affairs of the land in the next ten months and thereafter rule the land bringing everyone under one umbrella of his rule. Those who could not understand the power of Lord Vaikundar called his proclamation as sputter and twaddle. Unmindful of the comments, Lord Vaikundar kept himself cool and decided to examine the state of affairs staying as one amongst the folks. He sat in penance for six years spread in three phases, seeking grace from Lord Shiva. The first for the destruction of the Kali yuga and dawn of Dharma Yuga, the second for the removal of caste barriers and emergence of good creatures, and lastly the third for the well-being of the Saptha Kannigas along with upliftment of Sandroars. The then Poovandar Thoppu has been the present land of Swami Thoppu the northern gate area of which is where Lord Vaikundar sat in penance. The Swamithoppu Pathi has been referred as Manavai Pathi and Tamaraiyur Pathi in Akilathirattu. 
<br /><br />
In Swamithoppu Lord Vaikundar stayed in the form of a mendicant and when he wished that the folks of fifty-six countries come to him, attracted by his mind voice, thousands of  folks from   eighteen castes like -Sanar, Idaiyar, Vanigar, Nanatha Kaveri, Nalla Tulupattar, Shudra, Brahma, Tholvanigan, Paraiyan, Uthira Nisan, Uzhavan, Kuravan, Kammala Neelan, Karumaravan, Paravan, Vemma Naasuraani , Sangilian, Megavanna Idaiyaar etc flocked to Swamithoppu every day where Lord Vaikundar established land of equality by making people belonging to all castes draw water from the same well for bathe and daily chores including drinking purpose.
<br /><br />
When the philosophy of Lord Vaikundar spread across the land, people belonging to various castes began to come there, surrendered and offered worship to him. Lord Vaikundar told everyone that as prophesied in the Vedas, he had incarnated  in Kaliyuga to establish rule of Dharma and the pious people reborn would witness the change. He appealed to the folks to surrender at the feet of the Lord firmly who was the creator and protector of the universe. All should live in harmony, remain firm adhering to basic rituals and loyal to the Lord.  His teachings were directed not only to the humans, but also meant to the birds, animals and all other living beings including plants and trees, reptiles, rough rocks and stones which were nonliving beings (There exist some form of life in the static beings too). The folks firmly believed that Lord Narayana has indeed incarnated as Lord Vaikundar as fore told by Veda Vyasa in the Veda Agama and therefore surrendered at his feet. They also firmly believed that the path had been laid for the destruction of those opposed to Lord Narayana.  
<br /><br />
At the end of the penance, the folks went to Lord Vaikundar and lamented that they were unable to bear the atrocities of evil demons and spirits under influence of Kalian. On hearing their woes, Lord Vaikundar quickly visualized what had happened and the extent of atrocities committed by evil Kali.  After evaluating the past atrocities of the evil forces and reconciled with the rules in Agamas, it became clear to him that the time to completely destroy the powers of the demonic forces had ripened. Therefore, he quickly acted to curb them in the next few minutes. He destroyed the powers of the evil ghosts and spirits and renamed five evil warriors called Pancha Devas or Guardian deities -Sudalaimadan, Kattayeri, Palavesam, Masanam, and Kathavarayan- as Karudarasan, Similarasan, Thertagan, and Tuttiveeran and as Therkudayon. Their divine powers were replaced with modified powers since they had earlier accepted the animal sacrifices against the dictums of the Lord. Thereafter he kept them by his right side in the name of ‘Yeval Seevaai’ (meaning the one who obeyed his command). Those developments were viewed by Akilathirattu as 'transformation of the status of the demi gods’. In the act of next, Lord Vaikundar decided to snatch away the powers of evil magicians engaged in the art of black magic, sorcery etc., and sent wild animals to attack them. The magicians could not contain the anger of those wild animals with their magical powers and therefore on the advice of a lady fowler (who forecast the future in trance), they surrendered at the feet of Lord Vaikundar assuring that they would henceforth never indulge in the art of evil black magic or sorcery. The folk of the land felt happy and heaved sigh of relief over the development.
<br /><br />
Severe dress code existed in the rule of Kaliyarasan (King) to those belonging to the oppressed and suppressed folks. The male folks were not allowed to wear turban, robes or allowed to cover their upper body or wear dhotis extending below knees touching toes.  Similarly, the females were not allowed to cover their upper body. Hence Lord Vaikundar advised that those norms be defied, males should come wearing turban or a head gear and similarly the females should cover their full body with cloth (Sari). The Lord prophesied that ‘in future there would only be givers and not takers. If the folks desired re-establishment of Yuga of Dharma, then they should not only love each other, but also desist from practicing animal sacrifices with sheep, goats and chickens. 
<br /><br />
In order to eradicate the evil practice of untouchability, he picked up sacred clay (Thiru Namam) from the ground and personally applied it in the form of a mark on the forehead of everyone. In those days every caste had their own community well to fetch water for use for all purposes. Folks of other community were not allowed to use them. In order to establish oneness of feeling in all, he enforced that everyone, irrespective of their caste should fetch water only from one well available in the place of his stay for use of daily chores. This practice of using water from the common well promoted qualities of equality, social justice, self-respect, fearlessness, and life of virtue. Lord Vaikundar settled in a small town called Manavai Pathi also called Tamaraiyur Pathi in the southern end. 
<br /><br />
As Lord Vaikundar continued to perform wonder social transformations, five of the folks became his closest disciples. They were Shri. Sivanandi, Shri. Pandaram, Shri. Alagesan, Shri. Subbaiah and Shri. Arigopalan, all of whom carried title Citars, were none but Dharma, Bhima, Arjuna, Nakula and Sahadeva, the five Pancha Pandava brothers in the past birth in Dwapara Yuga. Hence Lord Vaikundar made them get birth in Kaliyuga to become his disciples. One amongst the five was Shri. Arigopalan Citar who scripted Holy Akilathirattu the so-called Veda Agama.
<br /><br />
As each day passed, when folks of different castes, attracted by his charisma, and the teachings continued to flock around Lord Vaikundar, it became eye sore to the upper caste people. The ruler was also concerned that a separate socialistic state of society was slowly and steadily getting firmly established in Swamithoppu. Failed to foresee the personal humiliation and disgrace awaiting the ruler, descendent from the flesh of Kroni, he decided to get Lord Vaikundar arrested. Prior to it, he called a Vedic pundit to analyze all aspects and forecast whether the planetary positions were really inimical or favorable to him (King).  After thoroughly analyzing the planetary positions and other astrological aspects, the pundit opined that as per his reading, the effect of Kali has spread far and wide to seven worlds because of which infuriated Lord Maha Vishnu was set to take birth as human to destroy Kali and to bring everyone under the umbrella of one rule called Dharma, further indicating that the ripe time had already come for such a change.
<br /><br />
But the unconvinced ruler wondered whether the Lord would incarnate in the most downtrodden caste of Sanar instead of taking birth in upper castes like Potthi, Namboothri and Brahmin. In an act of haste, the ruler directed his Minister to immediately proceed to get the Lord arrested. Sensing trouble, an elderly person (Poovandar) from the shepherd clan told the ruler that Lord Maha Vishnu does not discriminate anyone based on caste as he was creator of the universe and would only confine his acts to the purpose for which he had incarnated. It was undoubtedly clear that the Kali Yuga had dawned and his incarnation certain to destroy Kali. He drew the ruler’s attention over the past when Lord Maha Vishnu appeared in the clan of Shepherds, Nomadic, and also as Visu and a Hunter.  Since the Lord pervades everywhere, he would never discriminate anyone and the mysterious acts of him cannot be understood. 
<br /><br />
