Those who came to Mutta Pathi continued to perform the thavasu (rituals) honestly. They took ritual bath for three times, wore washed bright whitish clothes unlike in the past, and their skin gilded like that of Lord Maha Vishnu, perhaps because Lord Maha Vishnu himself had incarnated in their clan. The other caste folks wondered how Lord Maha Vishnu, by incarnating amongst the Sanar folks, brought transformation in their life and inculcated the habit of eating food at the right time, and the art of praying with hymns.  They felt jealous that even the washed clothes of the upper class like Potti, Namboothri and Brahmins were not as bright as the Sanars who stayed with Lord Vaikundar. They could not digest the fact that the saris worn by the female folks of Sanar were sun bright compared to ladies of the upper castes. They grudged to notice the feelings of Sanars that they were no more mere workers who climb on the Palm and Coconut trees to fetch fruits to others.  After some time, Lord Maha Vishnu decided to send all of them back to their respective homes.
<br /><br />
