Later, Lord Maha Vishnu, by his mystic powers, summoned the seven maidens who had taken birth as Saasthirakanni, Kamalakanni, Mogakkanni, Moolakkanni, Kadungsakthi, Deivakkanni and Arimadavai. Once they arrived, he disguised himself into the form of a Pandaram groom, clad in Saffron robe, a garland made of Rudraksha bead adorning the neck, Thulsi leaves on his head, a stick and Kamandal (a water-vessel of a religious mendicant) in hand and tied the Mangal sutra (Wedding badge or string or thick yellow thread with a symbol knotted in the neck of bride) on their necks which he was carrying with him. While tying the knots, he blessed ‘May the land of Dharma flourish, let the Mangal sutra remain forever in the Yuga of Dharma, may the ladies and Sandroars live with dignity with the blessings of Lord Shiva and other divines. The place where he married the seven maidens is called Tamaraiyur Pathi also called Swamithoppu.  Further, Lord Maha Vishnu remarried goddess Seethalakshmi and as promised to the seven maidens earlier, he returned back the sons (delivered by them in previous Yuga) born to them. Everywhere folks were jubilant to see the events.
<br /><br />
<div style="padding-left:50px;">
tutiyitaik kanni marait tirumanan tirumal ceytu<br />
